orization of the outer loop within each thread's chunk? But with OpenMP, the loop is distributed among threads; each thread executes a contiguous subset. The compiler may vectorize that inner sequential loop over its assigned iterations if it can prove independence and no aliasing. With restrict pointers to SoA arrays, it might do so automatically at -O3 without explicit simd pragma. We could add `#pragma omp simd` before the outer loop inside parallel region? Actually we can use:\n\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n  #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n    ...\n  }\n}\n\nand then also put `#pragma omp simd` on that same loop? That would be invalid because it's already a worksharing loop. The correct way is to use `#pragma omp for simd schedule(static)` which combines both: the loop iterations are distributed among threads and each thread executes its portion in SIMD mode. This is standard OpenMP 4.0+. GCC supports it. So we can write:\n\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n  #pragma omp for simd schedule(static)\n  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n    // compute forces and update velocities\n  }\n}\n\nBut note: the inner j loop is still there, so each iteration of outer loop contains a long scalar loop. The `simd` clause tells compiler to vectorize the outer loop across iterations (multiple i at once). This should work.\n\nHowever, we must ensure that the reduction variables fx,fy,fz are private per i; they are local inside loop body, so fine.\n\nWe also need to handle position update similarly: can use `#pragma omp for simd` on the position loop too.\n\nSo plan:\n\n1. Change data layout from AoS to SoA internally.\n2. Read input into temporary Body array (or directly read floats into arrays? The binary format is exactly 6 floats per body; we could fread into a float buffer of size n*6, then split into px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz arrays). But easier: keep reading as before into Body*, then allocate separate arrays and copy.\n3. Modify body_force to take pointers to